  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nCurrent satellite and surface observations depict patchy dense fog\nforming in spots over northern lower, as well as stratus and \nfog persisting over eastern upper. Mid to high level clouds are \nmoving up and over an upper ridge (axis centered on WI this \nmorning). These clouds are approaching Lk MI, and will continue \nto the east today. Near the surface, KAPX VAD profiler shows 10 \nto 15kts of SE winds around a couple hundred feet up. These \nrelatively light, but more persistent winds have been keeping \nfog formation confined to the lower spots for northern lower \nwhile the higher hills remain mostly clear. The mid to high\nclouds will also work to keep widespread fog and stratus out of \nNW lower. As a more mature stratus/fog deck forms near the \nnorthern Saginaw Bay region and up to Thunder Bay, this will \nlikely drift to the west, bringing dense fog to the interior higher\nhills of northern lower by sunrise. Patchy low stratus and fog \nwill linger through the late morning, with visibilities \nimproving by noon. \n\nToday, the upper ridge axis will move over northern MI. At the \nsame time, WAA over the northern plains will approach MI, \nhowever the CWA will likely remain on the fringes. High \ntemperatures will be slightly warmer than yesterday (reaching \ninto the 60s again), with light south winds. The larger upper \npattern begins to shift Sunday, with a shortwave trough \nflattening the upper ridge towards the Ohio River Valley. A \ncold front will gradually move across the state from NW to SE \nSunday. Chances for weak rain showers exist over eastern upper \nstarting Sunday morning. These chances drift south over northern\nlower later Sunday, with very low chances for anything over a \ntenth of an inch. A wide temperature gradient will exist Sunday \nas cooler air keeps high temperatures in the upper 50s for much \nof eastern upper, with low 70s expected for much of northern \nlower which will see lighter southwest winds for most of the \nday.\n\nSunday night into Monday, a 1035mb surface high will continue to \nseep over northern MI. High temperatures will remain in the 40s and \n50s over all of northern MI with mostly cloudy skies. A couple short \nwaves will likely miss northern MI to the north during this time, \nhowever chances for a shortwave to move over northern MI\nincrease for the later half of the work week.",
